.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt TCP 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tcp
.Nd the Transmission Control Protocol
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.In netinet/in.h
.In netinet/tcp.h
.Ft int
.Fn socket "AF_INET" "SOCK_STREAM" "0"
.Ft int
.Fn socket "AF_INET6" "SOCK_STREAM" "0"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A
.Dv SOCK_STREAM
socket of
.Dv AF_INET
or
.Dv AF_INET6
is a TCP connection: a reliable stream of bytes in each direction between
two ports.
.Xr connect 2
opens one,
.Xr listen 2
and
.Xr accept 2
take those a peer opens, and
.Xr shutdown 2
and
.Xr close 2
end them.
.Xr connect 2
gives the retransmission timeout, the handshake's give-up and the resets.
A send during the handshake waits for its end, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
on a nonblocking socket, as
.Xr send 2
says.
.Ss Ports and TIME_WAIT
A socket that binds no port is given one of the ephemeral range, 32768
through 60999, Linux's
.Pa ip_local_port_range .
A bind to port zero and a listen without a port take a port no connection,
listener or bound socket has, trying the odd ports first from a place no
other host can predict.
A connection takes the first even port, then odd, from where the last
connection to the same peer address and port left off, chosen by a keyed
hash of that destination, as Linux does (RFC 6056, algorithm 4): a port
held by a listener or a bound socket is passed over, and one another
connection has is taken only for a different peer, so each destination has
the whole range.
.Pp
The side that closes first holds the connection in
.Dv TIME_WAIT
for 60 seconds, Linux's length, which keeps its port from the next
connection to the same peer; a FIN the peer sends again is acknowledged
and starts the 60 seconds again.
A connection there keeps no buffers.
The connections in
.Dv TIME_WAIT
are at most Linux's
.Pa tcp_max_tw_buckets
for the machine's memory, half a power of two near one for each 128 KiB,
from 256 to 262144; past that a connection skips
.Dv TIME_WAIT ,
counted in TCPTimeWaitOverflow.
.Ss Acknowledgements
Data received in order is acknowledged as Linux acknowledges it.
The connection's first data, and data after a quiet time longer than its
retransmission timeout, grant up to 16 quick acknowledgements, one for each
two full segments the window last offered holds, or 2 when it holds fewer
than two:
while any are left, and the connection is not answering what it receives,
each segment is acknowledged at once.
Data the peer sends again, and data past the window, a probe of a closed
one among it, grant them too and end the answering mode.
Otherwise its acknowledgement waits while no more than a full segment is
unacknowledged, so that every second full segment is acknowledged at once;
a held acknowledgement goes with the next segment sent, or alone 40 ms
after the first segment held.
Linux's delayed acknowledgement timeout adapts and can be longer; 40 ms,
Linux's
.Dv TCP_DELACK_MIN ,
is the longest one is held here.
A full segment is the largest received, at least 536 bytes; two shorter
segments in a row whose length with the header is the same, at least 556
bytes, or 108 without FIN, SYN, URG or PSH, make that length less 20 the
full one, whatever options each carries.
.Pp
A read that empties the receive buffer after a segment that ended a write
of the peer's sends the held acknowledgement at once.
A full segment ends a write when it has PSH, and a shorter one unless it is
the second of a new full length.
When the connection is answering what it receives, a read sends one only
after two such segments: data sent within 40 ms of data received puts it in
that mode, and an acknowledgement held for the whole 40 ms ends the mode.
Data past a hole, the segment that fills it, data received before, a FIN,
and data in a closing state are acknowledged at once.
.Pp
Every segment with ACK and without SYN before TIME_WAIT offers the window
as it stands when it is sent: while less than half the buffer is free, less than a sixteenth of it
or than a full segment is offered as zero, no window offered is taken back,
and the quick acknowledgements end, as Linux's
.Fn __tcp_select_window
offers it.
In TIME_WAIT an acknowledgement offers the window saved on entering it,
even after a read and when the first acknowledgement of the FIN was not
sent.
A read announces the window it reopened only while the peer has no more
than half the buffer left, and only when the window as it stands, in whole
units of the window scale and at least a full segment and a sixteenth of
the largest window, is at least twice what the peer has, as Linux's
.Fn tcp_cleanup_rbuf
announces it.
.Ss Keepalive
A connection with
.Dv SO_KEEPALIVE
set, nothing queued and nothing unacknowledged that has received neither
an acceptable acknowledgement nor data in order for
.Dv TCP_KEEPIDLE
seconds is sent a probe: one byte the peer has had already, at the
sequence number before the oldest unacknowledged one, which a peer that is
there answers with an acknowledgement.
While none is answered another goes each
.Dv TCP_KEEPINTVL
seconds, and when
.Dv TCP_KEEPCNT
probes have gone unanswered the peer is sent a reset and the connection
ends with
.Er ETIMEDOUT .
Only a segment that passes the checks of
.Sx Segments refused
and acknowledges something from the oldest unacknowledged byte to the
next to be sent answers the probes, as on Linux.
A segment refused there, one with an older acknowledgement, and one
outside the receive window answer none; in-order data with an older
acknowledgement, or a new FIN on bytes received before, restarts the
idle time but keeps the count of probes.
The defaults, 7200 seconds, 75 seconds and 9 probes, are those of
.Pa /proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_keepalive_time ,
.Pa tcp_keepalive_intvl
and
.Pa tcp_keepalive_probes ;
a socket's own values replace them, and a new value applies at once,
measured from the last acknowledgement or data that restarted the idle
time.
A connection
.Xr accept 2
gives takes the listener's settings.
A probe from the peer, a segment with no data below the next sequence
number expected, is answered with an acknowledgement, as any segment
outside the receive window is; see
.Sx Segments refused .
.Ss Give-up
A connection whose data or FIN goes unacknowledged is given up with
.Er ETIMEDOUT
when Linux would give it up with
.Pa /proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_retries2
of 15: after the time 16 timeouts take when the first is 200 milliseconds
and each doubles up to 120 seconds, 924.6 seconds, counted, as on Linux,
from the first retransmission of the loss, a fast retransmission or a
timeout's, until an acknowledgement ends the recovery.
The first timeout since data was last acknowledged never gives a
connection up.
This side's own timeout doubles only up to 60 seconds, so it sends more
retransmissions in that time than Linux does.
A connection whose local address goes, by a change of address or its
removal, is kept, as on Linux: each segment it sends is refused for want
of a route and counted lost, and its retransmissions resume the connection
when the address returns.
Given up first, it reports
.Er ENETUNREACH
in place of
.Er ETIMEDOUT .
A peer window that stays closed over data waiting to be sent, with
nothing in flight, is probed with an acknowledgement of no data below the
oldest byte unacknowledged, at the timeout, doubled after each probe up to
120 seconds; once
.Pa tcp_retries2
probes in a row have gone unanswered, the connection is given up with
.Er ETIMEDOUT ,
counted in TCPAbortOnTimeout, as on Linux: 924.6 seconds after the window
closed when the timeout is 200 milliseconds.
A peer that answers each probe with its window still closed keeps the
connection open.
A window closed over data in flight is probed at each retransmission
timeout instead, which then counts in no TCPTimeouts and lowers no segment
size, by sending up to a segment of the oldest data unacknowledged past the
closed window, counted in TCPRetransSegs, which a peer whose window has
opened takes; the connection is given up only once no acknowledgement
inside the data sent, a duplicate among them, in a segment inside the
window this side offers, has been received for 240 seconds before a
timeout and the probes have lasted as long, as on Linux.
Data that starts at or past the right edge of the window this side
offered, a closed window's included, is dropped and acknowledged.
.Dv TCP_USER_TIMEOUT ,
a count of milliseconds, replaces that time, in the handshake as well,
ends the keepalive's probing that long after the last acknowledgement or
data that restarted its idle time,
and ends a closed window's probing that long after its first probe,
answered or not, or over data in flight, just past that long after the
loss's first retransmission, as Linux does; 0 restores the default.
When the first probe over data in flight is that first retransmission,
it gives up at once if that long has passed since the oldest data
unacknowledged was first sent, as on Linux.
A frame the card refuses is kept and sent again at the next tick; a
connection whose every frame the card has refused for 30 seconds, with
nothing sent between, is given up, but the wait does not run while the card
reports its link down, so a cable out for a minute ends nothing.
.Ss Segments refused
After the handshake and before
.Dv TIME_WAIT ,
a connection judges each segment before it takes anything from it, as
Linux does.
One without the ACK flag is dropped.
One outside the receive window, which ends before the data this side last
acknowledged or starts past the right edge of the window it offered, is
dropped and answered with an acknowledgement of what was received: one
carrying data or a FIN each time, and one without, as a peer's keepalive
probe is, at most once in 500 milliseconds, as Linux's
.Pa tcp_invalid_ratelimit
has it, so that two ends that each find the other's segments stale do
not answer each other for ever.
Its acknowledgement and window are not taken.
A SYN draws a challenge acknowledgement, which a true peer answers with a
segment this side accepts (RFC 5961, section 4).
An acknowledgement below the oldest unacknowledged byte by more than the
largest window the peer has offered or the bytes it has acknowledged,
whichever is less, drops the segment with a challenge in every state
(RFC 5961, section 5); the bytes acknowledged count this side's SYN only on
an active open, as on Linux.
An older acknowledgement nearer is taken without its window.
Only then is an acknowledgement at or past the oldest unacknowledged byte
judged against the data sent: one of data never sent drops the segment,
with a challenge after the peer's FIN or this side's.
A segment with data or a FIN that the peer sent again after it was taken
whole, because this side's acknowledgement was lost, is acknowledged and
counted in DelayedACKLost, from the handshake's end to
.Dv LAST_ACK ,
but in
.Dv CLOSING
and
.Dv LAST_ACK
not when it acknowledges this side's FIN, which ends the connection first;
.Dv TIME_WAIT
acknowledges only a FIN sent again and counts nothing.
After the peer's FIN, a segment whose data runs past that FIN ends the
connection as an accepted reset does and is answered with a reset,
counted in TCPAbortOnData, as on Linux.
Before the peer's FIN, once this side's FIN is sent or queued after the
program's last close or a shutdown of both halves, data or a FIN past
what was received, which no program will read, ends the connection the
same way (RFC 1122, section 4.2.2.13); data and a FIN sent again are
acknowledged.
A close with received data the program has not read sends a reset in
place of the FIN and ends the connection, counted in TCPAbortOnClose, as
on Linux.
Until a passive open's handshake ends, the peer's SYN sent again is
answered with this side's SYN-ACK, an acknowledgement of another number
with a reset, a segment outside the window offered with an
acknowledgement, at most once in 500 milliseconds when it has a SYN or
neither data nor a FIN, and a SYN at another number with a reset that
ends the handshake.
Until a simultaneous open's handshake ends, a SYN draws a challenge
acknowledgement, but for the peer's SYN-ACK, a segment outside the
receive window a duplicate acknowledgement, and an acknowledgement of a
number other than this side's SYN or the one past it a reset; one of the
SYN's own number ends the handshake and leaves the SYN unacknowledged, to
be sent again as the SYN-ACK, as on Linux.
The acknowledgement that ends either handshake brings any data and FIN it
carries, which are taken at once.
Challenge acknowledgements share the limit of 500 milliseconds.
.Xr connect 2
gives the resets.
.Ss Black holes
A path that drops segments larger than some size without reporting it, a
black hole, is detected as RFC 4821 and Linux's
.Pa tcp_mtu_probing
1 detect it, when that mode is set.
The third timeout in a row of more than a floor of data, 536 bytes on IPv4
and 1220 on IPv6, sent on a link that is up, lowers the segment size to 1024
bytes, and each further timeout halves it, never below the floor.
Ten minutes later a probe, a segment halfway between the size and the
largest the peer and the path allow, goes ahead of new data when enough is
queued; its acknowledgement makes its size the connection's and the next
probe goes at once, and its loss lowers the top of the search below it.
A search that has closed to within 8 bytes starts again from the top ten
minutes later.
.Pa /proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_mtu_probing
sets the mode: 0, the default, does neither, since a stall of a radio link
times out as a black hole does, 1 starts the search at a black hole, and 2
starts every connection at 1024 bytes and probes upward.
A router's report that a segment was too big lowers the size and the top of
the search at once.
.Ss Options
At level
.Dv SOL_SOCKET ,
.Dv SO_KEEPALIVE
turns keepalive on with a nonzero integer and off with 0.
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_TCP :
.Bl -tag -width TCP_USER_TIMEOUT
.It Dv TCP_KEEPIDLE
The idle seconds before the first probe, 1 to 32767.
.It Dv TCP_KEEPINTVL
The seconds between probes, 1 to 32767.
.It Dv TCP_KEEPCNT
The probes unanswered before the reset, 1 to 127.
.It Dv TCP_QUICKACK
0 puts the connection in the mode in which this side's answers carry its
acknowledgements.
Any other value ends that mode and, when an acknowledgement is held, does
what a read does after a segment that ended a write of the peer's: the
acknowledgement is sent when the receive buffer is empty or more than a
full segment is unacknowledged, or else the window is announced when a read
would announce it; then an even value puts the mode back.
With no acknowledgement held the mode stays ended.
It reads 0 in that mode and 1 otherwise, and on a socket with no connection
the mode is kept for the connection it opens.
.It Dv TCP_USER_TIMEOUT
The milliseconds of unacknowledged data after which the connection is given
up, 0 for the default.
.It Dv TCP_NODELAY
Accepted with no effect, since segments are sent as soon as the windows
allow.
.El
.Pp
Each is an integer, and
.Xr getsockopt 2
reads the keepalive values as the socket set them or as the defaults it
takes.
.Ss Counters
.Pa /proc/net/netstat
counts in its
.Ql TcpExt:
line the segments of data received again whole, DelayedACKLost; the
retransmission timeouts, TCPTimeouts; the connections ended by data no
program will read, TCPAbortOnData, those a close with data unread ended,
TCPAbortOnClose, and those a timeout ended, TCPAbortOnTimeout; the connections that skipped
.Dv TIME_WAIT
for a full table of them, TCPTimeWaitOverflow; the challenge acknowledgements sent, TCPChallengeACK,
and the SYNs that drew one, TCPSYNChallenge; the answers of a handshake
not yet ended held back by the limit, TCPACKSkippedSynRecv, those to a
segment outside the window, TCPACKSkippedSeq, and the challenges held
back, TCPACKSkippedChallenge; the keepalive probes sent,
TCPKeepAlive; and the probes of a larger segment lost and taken,
TCPMTUPFail and TCPMTUPSuccess.
.Pa /proc/net/snmp
counts the segments and the connections ended; see
.Xr proc 5 .
.Ss Errors
.Xr setsockopt 2
fails with
.Er EINVAL
for a value shorter than an integer or out of the option's range.
A connection given up reports
.Er ETIMEDOUT
to a blocked call,
.Dv SO_ERROR
and
.Xr poll 2 ,
or
.Er ENETUNREACH
when a segment was refused for want of a route since the last
acknowledgement, as when its local address is gone.
